VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L1 — Complex capital structure definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which of the following describes a complex capital structure?', '["A structure with more than one class of common stock issued", "A structure where total debt exceeds total equity", "A structure that includes securities that could potentially dilute basic EPS", "A structure with both common and preferred stock outstanding"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding securities that could potentially dilute earnings per share, such as convertible bonds, stock options, or warrants. Simply having multiple stock classes (choice A) or preferred stock (choice D) does not alone create a complex capital structure unless those securities have dilutive potential.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — RSU effect on diluted EPS via treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Glenfield Corp. has net income of $1,200,000, 400,000 weighted-average shares outstanding, and 15,000 unvested restricted stock units (RSUs) that vest over three years. During 2025, Glenfield recognized $180,000 in compensation expense for these RSUs, and the average unrecognized compensation cost was $240,000. The average market price was $60 per share. What is the most likely effect of these RSUs on Glenfield''s diluted EPS computation?', '["All 15,000 RSU shares are added to the denominator with no numerator adjustment", "The treasury stock method is applied using unrecognized compensation as assumed proceeds, adding fewer than 15,000 incremental shares", "RSUs are excluded from diluted EPS until they fully vest", "The if-converted method is applied, adding back compensation expense to the numerator"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28, unvested RSUs are treated as options under the treasury stock method. The assumed proceeds include unrecognized compensation cost, so not all 15,000 shares are incremental—only the net shares after hypothetical buyback at the average market price. Choice A incorrectly adds all shares without applying the treasury stock method. Choice C is wrong because unvested RSUs are included in diluted EPS before vesting.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Two-class method for participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Briarcliff Industries has net income of $900,000 and 200,000 common shares outstanding. The company also has 50,000 shares of participating preferred stock that receive dividends equal to common shareholders on a per-share basis after a $2 per share preferred dividend is paid. Total declared dividends are $500,000. Under the two-class method, how does the participation feature most likely affect basic EPS?', '["The preferred dividends of $100,000 are deducted from the numerator, and the remaining income is divided by 200,000 shares", "All income is allocated to common shares because participating rights apply only to diluted EPS", "The preferred stock is treated as converted to common, increasing the denominator by 50,000 shares", "After deducting declared dividends, undistributed earnings are allocated between common and preferred shareholders based on their participation rights"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, the two-class method requires undistributed earnings to be allocated between common and participating securities based on their respective rights to share in earnings. This reduces the numerator available to common shareholders for basic EPS. Choice A ignores the allocation of undistributed earnings to participating preferred. Choice C describes the if-converted method, not the two-class method.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Definition of dilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'In the context of EPS under ASC 260, what does the term ''dilutive'' mean?', '["A potential common share that would decrease EPS if converted or exercised", "A potential common share that would increase EPS if converted or exercised", "Any outstanding security convertible into common stock", "A decrease in the market price of common stock during the period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, a dilutive security is one that would reduce earnings per share (or increase loss per share) if exercised or converted. Only dilutive securities are included in the diluted EPS computation; antidilutive securities are excluded. Choice B describes an antidilutive security, which is the opposite of dilutive and must be excluded from diluted EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Mid-period convertible bond issuance effect on diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinehurst Corp. issues $1,000,000 face value of 8% convertible bonds on July 1, 2025. Each $1,000 bond converts into 20 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. Pinehurst had 300,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $750,000 for the full year. When computing diluted EPS under the if-converted method, how does the mid-year issuance affect the calculation?', '["The full year''s interest is added back to the numerator, and all 20,000 convertible shares are added to the denominator", "No adjustment is needed because the bonds were outstanding less than a full year", "Half the year''s after-tax interest ($30,000) is added to the numerator, and 10,000 shares (weighted for six months) are added to the denominator", "The full year''s after-tax interest is added to the numerator, but only 10,000 shares are added to the denominator"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method assumes conversion at the later of issuance or the beginning of the period. Since the bonds were issued July 1, only six months of after-tax interest ($1,000,000 x 8% x 6/12 x 0.75 = $30,000) is added to the numerator, and only 10,000 shares (20,000 x 6/12) are added to the denominator. Choice A incorrectly uses full-year amounts without weighting.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Post-period stock split retroactive adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redstone Inc. reports basic EPS of $3.50 for the year ended December 31, 2025, based on 500,000 weighted-average shares. On February 15, 2026, before the financial statements are issued, Redstone declares a 2-for-1 stock split. How should this stock split affect the EPS reported in the 2025 financial statements?', '["The stock split is disclosed in the notes only and does not affect the reported EPS figures", "The weighted-average shares should be retroactively adjusted to 1,000,000, reducing reported basic EPS to $1.75", "The stock split is treated as a subsequent event requiring no adjustment to 2025 EPS", "The 2025 EPS is reported at $3.50, with a supplemental pro forma EPS of $1.75 disclosed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends and stock splits occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statements are issued must be given retroactive recognition in the EPS computation. The weighted-average shares are adjusted from 500,000 to 1,000,000, reducing basic EPS to $1.75. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 requires retroactive adjustment, not mere disclosure.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Weighted-average shares for business combination
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Clearwater Holdings acquires Summerlin Corp. on September 1, 2025, issuing 60,000 shares of common stock as consideration. Clearwater had 240,000 shares outstanding at January 1, 2025, with no other share activity. What is Clearwater''s weighted-average number of shares for 2025?', '["300,000", "280,000", "240,000", "260,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, shares issued as purchase consideration in a business combination are included in the weighted-average from the acquisition date. Clearwater includes 240,000 shares for the full year plus 60,000 shares for 4/12 months (September through December): 240,000 + 20,000 = 260,000. Choice A (300,000) incorrectly includes all 60,000 shares for the entire year rather than weighting from the acquisition date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Warrants diluted EPS calculation via treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hawthorne Inc. has 150,000 weighted-average common shares, net income of $600,000, and 8,000 warrants outstanding with an exercise price of $35. The average market price during 2025 was $50. Using the treasury stock method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$3.94", "$4.00", "$3.80", "$3.70"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes warrants are exercised and proceeds repurchase shares at the average market price. Proceeds: 8,000 x $35 = $280,000; shares repurchased: $280,000 / $50 = 5,600; incremental shares: 2,400. Diluted EPS: $600,000 / 152,400 = $3.94. Choice C ($3.80) incorrectly adds all 8,000 warrant shares to the denominator without applying the treasury stock method.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Entities required to present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the income statement?', '["All entities with more than one class of common stock", "All entities that prepare financial statements in conformity with GAAP", "Only entities whose common stock or potential common stock is publicly traded", "Only entities with annual revenues exceeding $10 million"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock traded on a public market. Nonpublic entities are exempt from the EPS presentation requirement. Choice B is incorrect because many GAAP-compliant private companies are not required to present EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Participating securities in net loss — two-class method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornridge Corp. reports a net loss of $400,000 for 2025 with 100,000 common shares and 25,000 participating preferred shares outstanding. The preferred shares contractually participate in distributions equally with common on a per-share basis. Under the two-class method, how does the net loss affect the allocation between common and preferred for basic EPS?', '["The net loss is allocated 80% to common and 20% to preferred based on relative share counts", "The entire net loss is allocated to common shareholders because participating securities do not share in losses unless contractually obligated to do so", "The net loss is split equally per share between common and preferred", "Net losses are never allocated under the two-class method"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-66, when applying the two-class method in a net loss period, losses are not allocated to participating securities unless those securities have a contractual obligation to share in the entity''s losses. Unless the participation feature explicitly requires loss sharing, the entire loss goes to common. Choice A is incorrect because automatic pro rata loss allocation requires an explicit contractual obligation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred if-converted numerator adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Mountainview Corp. has net income of $2,000,000, 500,000 common shares, and 20,000 shares of $50 par, 7% convertible preferred stock. Each preferred share converts into 3 common shares. Preferred dividends of $70,000 were declared. Using the if-converted method, what is the numerator for diluted EPS?', '["$2,000,000", "$1,930,000", "$2,070,000", "$1,860,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method assumes convertible preferred shares were converted at the beginning of the period. The $70,000 preferred dividend deducted for basic EPS is added back, making the diluted EPS numerator $2,000,000 (full net income). Choice B ($1,930,000) is the basic EPS numerator and incorrectly fails to reverse the preferred dividend deduction when assuming conversion.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Sequencing: options affect subsequent bond dilution test
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Brookdale Inc. has basic EPS of $5.00 (net income $1,000,000, 200,000 shares). After including 4,000 incremental shares from stock options, EPS declines to $4.90. The company also has $200,000 of 5% convertible bonds (tax rate 25%) convertible into 8,000 shares. After including options first, what is the status of the convertible bonds?', '["The bonds are antidilutive because their per-share impact exceeds basic EPS of $5.00", "The order of inclusion has no effect on the bonds'' dilution status", "The bonds are excluded because convertible bonds are always tested before options", "The bonds are dilutive because their per-share impact of $0.94 is below the running EPS of $4.90 after including options"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, securities are tested from most to least dilutive. The bonds'' per-share impact is $7,500 / 8,000 = $0.94 (interest: $200,000 x 5% x 0.75 = $7,500). Because $0.94 is below the running EPS of $4.90 after including options, the bonds further reduce EPS and remain dilutive. Choice A incorrectly compares the per-share impact to basic EPS rather than the running diluted figure.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — EPS presentation for discontinued operations
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbridge Corp. has income from continuing operations of $800,000 and a loss from discontinued operations of $200,000 (net of tax), with 250,000 weighted-average common shares and no preferred stock. What EPS amounts must Westbridge present?', '["Only a single EPS figure of $2.40 for net income", "EPS of $3.20 for continuing operations on the income statement, with no requirement to report EPS for discontinued operations", "EPS of $3.20 for continuing operations and $(0.80) for discontinued operations", "EPS of $2.40 for net income and $3.20 for gross profit"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, entities must present basic and diluted EPS for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement. Per-share amounts for discontinued operations must also be disclosed. Continuing operations EPS: $800,000 / 250,000 = $3.20; discontinued: $(200,000) / 250,000 = $(0.80). Choice A is incorrect because a single net income figure omits the required separate continuing operations EPS.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: easy / L2 — Calculate incremental shares for warrants
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestview Corp. has 5,000 warrants with an exercise price of $30 and an average market price of $50. Using the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares are added for diluted EPS?', '["5,000", "2,000", "3,000", "3,500"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes warrants are exercised and proceeds repurchase shares at the average market price. Proceeds: 5,000 x $30 = $150,000; repurchased shares: $150,000 / $50 = 3,000; incremental shares: 5,000 - 3,000 = 2,000. Choice C (3,000) incorrectly reports the number of shares repurchased rather than the net incremental shares added.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — If-converted vs. two-class method for convertible participating preferred
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashford Holdings has 300,000 common shares, net income of $1,800,000, and 40,000 shares of $25 par, 6% convertible preferred stock that participates equally with common on a per-share basis. Each preferred share converts into 2 common shares. Preferred dividends of $60,000 were declared. Evaluate whether Ashford should use the if-converted method or the two-class method for basic EPS and determine which produces the more dilutive result.', '["The if-converted method produces lower basic EPS because adding 80,000 shares to the denominator outweighs the two-class numerator allocation, and ASC 260 requires using the more dilutive result", "The two-class method always takes precedence over the if-converted method for convertible securities", "Both methods produce identical results for convertible participating preferred stock", "The two-class method produces lower basic EPS because allocating undistributed earnings to preferred reduces the common numerator more than adding shares reduces the if-converted denominator"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, when preferred stock is both convertible and participating, the entity must compute basic EPS under both methods and report the more dilutive result. The if-converted method divides full net income by 380,000 shares (approximately $4.74), while the two-class method allocates undistributed earnings but divides only by 300,000 shares (approximately $5.13). The if-converted method is more dilutive. Choice D is incorrect because dividing by fewer shares under the two-class method typically produces higher EPS.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Contingently issuable shares: conditions not met
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Edgemont Corp. has an agreement to issue 10,000 additional shares to a former owner if net income for the year ending December 31, 2025, exceeds $5,000,000. Actual net income for 2025 is $4,200,000. How should these contingent shares be treated in Edgemont''s 2025 EPS computations?', '["The 10,000 shares are included in both basic and diluted EPS because the contingency will likely be met", "The 10,000 shares are included in basic EPS only, weighted from the agreement date", "The 10,000 shares are included in diluted EPS only, weighted from the beginning of the period", "The 10,000 shares are excluded from both basic and diluted EPS because the earnings contingency has not been met"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares based on an earnings target are included in basic EPS only when the conditions are met at the reporting date. Since net income of $4,200,000 does not exceed $5,000,000, the shares are excluded from basic EPS. For diluted EPS, the current period''s earnings are the assumed level, and $4,200,000 still falls short. Choice A incorrectly includes shares based on probability rather than conditions actually being met.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Definition of weighted-average common shares outstanding
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What does the term ''weighted-average common shares outstanding'' mean in the context of EPS?', '["The total number of authorized shares at year-end", "The highest number of shares outstanding during the period", "The number of shares outstanding adjusted for the time each share was outstanding during the period", "The simple average of beginning and ending shares outstanding"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding reflects shares adjusted for the portion of the period during which they were outstanding. Shares issued mid-year are weighted for only the fraction of the year they were outstanding. Choice D is incorrect because a simple average of beginning and ending balances does not properly account for the specific dates of share issuances and repurchases.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Multiple securities sequencing and overall dilution assessment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Industries has net income of $2,400,000, 400,000 weighted-average shares, $40,000 in preferred dividends, and three potentially dilutive securities: (1) 12,000 stock options producing 3,600 incremental treasury-method shares, (2) $600,000 of 4% convertible bonds (tax rate 25%) convertible into 15,000 shares, and (3) 10,000 convertible preferred shares with $40,000 in dividends convertible into 18,000 shares. Recommend the proper sequencing for diluted EPS and assess whether all three securities should be included.', '["Sequence: bonds first, then preferred, then options — all three are dilutive", "Sequence: options first ($0 per-share impact), then bonds ($1.20), then preferred ($2.22) — all three are dilutive, producing diluted EPS of approximately $5.54", "Sequence: preferred first, then bonds, then options — the preferred is antidilutive", "Sequence: options first, then preferred, then bonds — the bonds are antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, securities are tested most dilutive first. Options ($0 impact) go first, then bonds ($18,000 / 15,000 = $1.20), then preferred ($40,000 / 18,000 = $2.22). After sequential inclusion, each security reduces EPS below the prior level, confirming all three are dilutive. Choice A misstates the required sequencing order, which must proceed from lowest to highest per-share impact.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Written put options on entity's own stock
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Selkirk Dynamics sold written put options on 20,000 of its own common shares with an exercise price of $45. The average market price during 2025 was $40, and the period-end price was $38. Selkirk''s basic EPS is $3.20 based on net income of $960,000 and 300,000 shares. Evaluate whether these written put options should be included in diluted EPS and determine their effect.', '["The written puts are dilutive because the exercise price exceeds the average market price, and the reverse treasury stock method adds incremental shares to the denominator", "The written puts are antidilutive and excluded because put options can never dilute EPS", "The written puts are dilutive only if actually exercised by the holder during the period", "The written puts are excluded because ASC 260 does not address put options on an entity''s own stock"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the average market price. The reverse treasury stock method assumes the entity repurchases shares at the exercise price ($45) and issues shares at the average market price ($40) to fund the repurchase, producing a net denominator increase. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 specifically requires dilutive written puts to be included in diluted EPS.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Treasury stock repurchase weighted-average effect
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakewood Industries has 500,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On August 1, 2025, Lakewood repurchases 60,000 shares as treasury stock. No other share transactions occur during the year. What is the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding for 2025?', '["440,000", "500,000", "460,000", "475,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, treasury stock repurchases reduce the weighted-average from the repurchase date. Lakewood had 500,000 shares for seven months and 440,000 shares for five months: (500,000 x 7/12) + (440,000 x 5/12) = 291,667 + 183,333 = 475,000. Choice A (440,000) incorrectly treats the repurchase as occurring on January 1, failing to weight for the actual date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: hard / L3 — Mandatorily convertible instruments EPS treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Sovereign Corp. issued $2,000,000 of mandatorily convertible subordinated notes on January 1, 2025, paying 5% annual interest. The notes automatically convert into 50,000 common shares on January 1, 2028. Sovereign has net income of $3,000,000, 600,000 common shares, and a 25% tax rate. Analyze how these mandatorily convertible notes affect both basic and diluted EPS for 2025.', '["The notes are excluded from both basic and diluted EPS because conversion is not until 2028", "The notes affect diluted EPS by adding 50,000 shares but no interest adjustment because conversion is mandatory", "The notes are included in diluted EPS only, adding $75,000 of after-tax interest to the numerator and 50,000 shares to the denominator under the if-converted method", "The notes are included in basic EPS by weighting the 50,000 shares from the issuance date"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, mandatorily convertible instruments are included in diluted EPS using the if-converted method. The after-tax interest of $75,000 ($2,000,000 x 5% x 0.75) is added to the numerator, and 50,000 conversion shares are added to the denominator. The notes are excluded from basic EPS because shares have not yet been issued. Choice B is incorrect because the interest add-back is required to avoid double-counting the instrument''s cost.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Year-to-date interim period basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Foxhaven Corp. reports quarterly earnings. For Q1 2025, net income was $200,000 with 100,000 weighted-average shares. For Q2, net income was $350,000 with 120,000 weighted-average shares (20,000 new shares issued April 1). What is year-to-date basic EPS through the second quarter?', '["$2.75", "$5.00", "$5.50", "$4.58"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, year-to-date EPS uses cumulative income divided by the year-to-date weighted-average shares. Cumulative income: $550,000. Year-to-date weighted-average: (100,000 x 3 + 120,000 x 3) / 6 = 110,000 shares. EPS: $550,000 / 110,000 = $5.00. Choice C ($5.50) incorrectly divides by only 100,000 shares, ignoring the mid-period share increase.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Numerator of basic EPS formula
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What is the numerator in the basic EPS formula under ASC 260?', '["Net income available to common shareholders (net income minus preferred dividends)", "Net income before income taxes", "Operating income", "Comprehensive income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-5, the numerator for basic EPS is net income available to common shareholders, calculated as net income minus preferred dividends (whether declared or, for cumulative preferred, accumulated). Choice B is incorrect because income taxes must be deducted before computing EPS, and the standard requires net income rather than pre-tax income.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Undeclared cumulative preferred dividends effect on basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, how are undeclared dividends on cumulative preferred stock treated in the basic EPS calculation?', '["They are ignored because dividends must be declared to affect EPS", "They are added to net income in the numerator", "They reduce the denominator by the number of preferred shares", "They are deducted from net income in the numerator even if not declared"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11A, for cumulative preferred stock, the period''s dividend is deducted from net income when computing basic EPS regardless of whether declared. Cumulative preferred stockholders have a claim on earnings that reduces the amount available to common shareholders. Choice A is incorrect because undeclared cumulative dividends still reduce the EPS numerator, unlike noncumulative preferred dividends.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Antidilutive sequencing: preferred becomes antidilutive after options and bonds
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Waverly Corp. has basic EPS of $2.00 ($560,000 income to common, 280,000 shares). Three potentially dilutive securities exist: (1) options producing 1,800 incremental shares, (2) $400,000 of 10% convertible bonds (tax rate 25%) convertible into 25,000 shares, and (3) convertible preferred with $40,000 in dividends convertible into 12,000 shares. Assess whether all three securities should be included in diluted EPS.', '["All three securities are dilutive and should be included in diluted EPS", "The stock options are antidilutive because they produce the fewest incremental shares", "The convertible preferred becomes antidilutive because its $3.33 per-share impact exceeds the running diluted EPS of $1.92", "The convertible bonds become antidilutive because the 10% coupon rate is too high"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, securities are tested most dilutive first. After including options (EPS approximately $1.99) and bonds (EPS approximately $1.92), the cumulative diluted EPS falls below the preferred''s per-share impact of $3.33 ($40,000 / 12,000). Adding preferred would increase EPS, making it antidilutive and excluded. Choice D is incorrect because the bonds'' per-share impact ($1.20) is below the running EPS, confirming they are dilutive.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Definition of treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what is the treasury stock method used for in computing diluted EPS?', '["Determining the tax benefit of repurchasing outstanding shares", "Calculating the incremental shares from stock options and warrants by assuming exercise proceeds are used to repurchase shares at the average market price", "Converting preferred stock into common stock for the diluted EPS denominator", "Accounting for actual treasury stock repurchases during the reporting period"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method applies to stock options and warrants. It assumes these instruments are exercised at the beginning of the period and the proceeds are used to repurchase common shares at the average market price, producing a net incremental share increase. Choice C describes the if-converted method, not the treasury stock method.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Performance-based options: conditions met
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Birchwood Corp. granted 10,000 performance-based stock options with an exercise price of $20 on January 1, 2025. The options vest only if revenue exceeds $50 million by December 31, 2025. Actual revenue is $53 million, and the average market price was $35. Using the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares are included in diluted EPS?', '["4,286 incremental shares", "10,000 incremental shares", "No incremental shares are added", "5,714 incremental shares"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28A, performance-based options where the condition has been met are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. Proceeds: 10,000 x $20 = $200,000; repurchased shares: $200,000 / $35 = 5,714; incremental shares: 10,000 - 5,714 = 4,286. Choice C is incorrect because performance options are included once the contingency is satisfied at the reporting date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Retroactive prior-year EPS adjustment for stock dividend
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinefield Corp. reported basic EPS of $6.00 for 2024 based on net income of $1,200,000 and 200,000 shares. On March 1, 2025, Pinefield declares a 20% stock dividend. When presenting comparative financial statements for 2024 and 2025, how does the stock dividend affect the 2024 EPS previously reported?', '["The 2024 EPS remains $6.00 with a footnote disclosing the 2025 stock dividend", "The 2024 EPS is restated only if the stock dividend is material", "The 2024 comparative EPS is adjusted prospectively from March 1, 2025", "The 2024 EPS is retroactively adjusted to $5.00 by recalculating with 240,000 shares to maintain comparability"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends are given retroactive treatment for all periods presented in comparative statements. The 2024 weighted-average shares are adjusted from 200,000 to 240,000 (200,000 x 1.20), reducing the 2024 EPS from $6.00 to $5.00 ($1,200,000 / 240,000). Choice A is incorrect because mere disclosure is insufficient—prior period EPS must be recalculated to preserve comparability.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Convertible debt with beneficial conversion feature (BCF) and diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hartwell Corp. issued $1,000,000 face value, three-year convertible notes on January 1, 2025, at par with a stated rate of 6% and a beneficial conversion feature (BCF) valued at $150,000 under ASC 470-20. The notes convert into 40,000 common shares. Hartwell has net income of $2,000,000, 500,000 common shares, and a 25% tax rate. Evaluate how the BCF affects the if-converted diluted EPS computation.', '["The BCF has no effect on diluted EPS because it is an equity component, and only the $45,000 cash interest after tax is added to the numerator", "The BCF eliminates the dilutive effect of the convertible notes by reducing their carrying amount", "The BCF increases the after-tax interest add-back to $82,500 because the discount amortization is additional interest expense reversed under the if-converted method", "The BCF requires the 40,000 conversion shares to be reduced proportionally to reflect the equity allocation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adds back all after-tax interest expense related to convertible debt, including amortization of any discount from a beneficial conversion feature. The BCF creates a $150,000 discount amortized over three years ($50,000/year), making total interest $110,000 and the after-tax add-back $82,500. Choice A incorrectly excludes the BCF amortization, which is recorded as interest expense and must be reversed.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Per-share amounts required on income statement face
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which per-share amounts must be presented on the face of the income statement?', '["Basic and diluted EPS for income from continuing operations and for net income", "Only basic EPS for net income", "Basic and diluted EPS for gross profit, operating income, and net income", "Basic EPS for all income statement subtotals"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, entities must present on the face of the income statement basic and diluted EPS for both income from continuing operations and net income. Presenting EPS for gross profit or operating income (choice C) is not required under ASC 260. Choice B is incorrect because both basic and diluted per-share amounts must be presented, not just basic.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
